<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!--
      The app theme, which on Android 12+ is also the splash screen: the platform draws
      `windowSplashScreenBackground` with `windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon` centred on it while the
      process starts. No androidx.core.splashscreen needed — minSdk is well past 31, so the platform
      API is always available.

      Colours are the design system's `canvas` token (values/colors.xml, Night; values-notnight, Day):
      a splash screen stands in for the app's first frame, and the app's first frame is drawn on `canvas`.

      The window background matches, so the hand-off from splash to the Compose UI is seamless instead
      of flashing the platform's default white. The rest of the app's colour comes from TanukiTheme in
      Compose — this only has to own the frames before Compose exists.

      The splash icon is the launcher foreground itself, not a copy: both need the mark inset to the
      central 66.67% of their canvas (an adaptive icon so the launcher mask can't clip it, a splash
      icon because the platform masks it to a circle at the same ratio), so one drawable satisfies
      both and they cannot drift apart.

      NoActionBar because the UI is a single edge-to-edge Compose surface with its own top bars.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.Tanuki" parent="@android:style/Theme.Material.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@color/tanuki_background</item>
        <item name="android:windowSplashScreenBackground">@color/tanuki_background</item>
        <item name="android:windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon">@drawable/ic_launcher_foreground</item>
    </style>
</resources>